WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.710 --> 00:00:07.810
- Hi everybody.

00:00:07.810 --> 00:00:09.460
I'm Maurice Samuels,

00:00:09.460 --> 00:00:11.310
the director of the Yale Program

00:00:11.310 --> 00:00:12.823
for the Study of Antisemitism,

00:00:14.008 --> 00:00:16.910
and I'm very happy to
welcome you here today

00:00:16.910 --> 00:00:20.160
to the final Benjamin and
Barbara Zucker lecture

00:00:20.160 --> 00:00:22.190
of the academic year.

00:00:22.190 --> 00:00:25.170
Today's speaker James or Jake McAuley,

00:00:25.170 --> 00:00:28.000
is a bit of a wonder cat.

00:00:28.000 --> 00:00:30.220
He earned his BA from Harvard,

00:00:30.220 --> 00:00:32.840
and his DPhil in history from Oxford

00:00:32.840 --> 00:00:34.920
where he was a Marshall Scholar.

00:00:34.920 --> 00:00:37.020
In his 30 short years

00:00:37.020 --> 00:00:38.910
he's already been the Paris correspondent

00:00:38.910 --> 00:00:40.190
for "The Washington Post,"

00:00:40.190 --> 00:00:44.400
and is now a global opinions
contributing columnists there.

00:00:44.400 --> 00:00:47.300
I'm happy to say also that
he's been invited next year

00:00:47.300 --> 00:00:49.290
to be a Franke fellow

00:00:49.290 --> 00:00:52.240
at the "Whitney Humanities
Center" at Yale.

00:00:52.240 --> 00:00:55.500
His new book, "The House
of Fragile Things,"

00:00:55.500 --> 00:00:59.140
about Jewish art collectors
and in fin-de-siècle France

00:00:59.140 --> 00:01:01.410
and the antisemitism they faced,

00:01:01.410 --> 00:01:03.647
which he's gonna be talking about today,

00:01:03.647 --> 00:01:06.800
was just published by
"Yale University Press"

00:01:06.800 --> 00:01:09.377
and it's gotten rave reviews.

00:01:09.377 --> 00:01:13.250
"The Wall Street Journal" called
it alluring and disturbing,

00:01:13.250 --> 00:01:15.539
which I think is a pretty good description

00:01:15.539 --> 00:01:18.950
of the complex world
that the book describes

00:01:18.950 --> 00:01:22.850
and the ambivalent emotions
that the book evokes.

00:01:22.850 --> 00:01:24.080
I just finished the book

00:01:24.080 --> 00:01:27.030
and I have to say that it
manages really brilliantly

00:01:27.030 --> 00:01:31.190
to be both scholarly
and extremely readable.

00:01:31.190 --> 00:01:32.700
It's a great read.

00:01:32.700 --> 00:01:35.740
So, here is how things
are gonna work today.

00:01:35.740 --> 00:01:38.130
Jake will speak for about 30 minutes,

00:01:38.130 --> 00:01:40.500
then my colleague Alice Kaplan,

00:01:40.500 --> 00:01:42.510
sterling professor of French

00:01:42.510 --> 00:01:45.410
and the director of the
"Whitney Humanities Center,"

00:01:45.410 --> 00:01:50.100
will kick off the discussion
and I will ask Jake questions

00:01:50.100 --> 00:01:53.200
that you submit using the Q&amp;A feature

00:01:53.200 --> 00:01:55.470
at the bottom of your screen.

00:01:55.470 --> 00:01:58.890
Feel free to submit questions
at any point during the talk,

00:01:58.890 --> 00:02:02.520
but please don't raise your
hand because we can't see you.

00:02:02.520 --> 00:02:04.140
So now without further ado,

00:02:04.140 --> 00:02:05.700
I will hand it over to Jake

00:02:05.700 --> 00:02:09.340
who I think has some slides also to show.

00:02:09.340 --> 00:02:10.193
- That's right.

00:02:11.130 --> 00:02:14.610
Thank you so much Maurice for
that very kind introduction,

00:02:14.610 --> 00:02:18.363
and it is terrific to be here
with you and Alice today,

00:02:19.450 --> 00:02:22.620
both of you helped me so much

00:02:22.620 --> 00:02:25.860
in more ways that I can
say here with the book

00:02:25.860 --> 00:02:28.410
as I was hustling to finish it

00:02:28.410 --> 00:02:32.080
over this past year of
lockdowns and so on.

00:02:32.080 --> 00:02:35.510
So, it's a pleasure to
be here with you today

00:02:35.510 --> 00:02:37.150
and thank you so much for having me

00:02:37.150 --> 00:02:41.310
and for all the kind words about the book.

00:02:41.310 --> 00:02:43.240
Which I'm happy to talk a little bit about

00:02:43.240 --> 00:02:44.900
with all of you today.

00:02:44.900 --> 00:02:47.010
So, as Maurice said,

00:02:47.010 --> 00:02:50.610
the book is called "The
House of Fragile Things."

00:02:50.610 --> 00:02:55.600
It is a study of a
beleaguered Jewish elite

00:02:55.600 --> 00:02:57.627
on the brink of catastrophe.

00:02:57.627 --> 00:03:02.627
So kind of following a network
of five intermarried families

00:03:02.660 --> 00:03:06.517
between the kind of with Dreyfus,

00:03:06.517 --> 00:03:09.230
the Dreyfus affair I should say,

00:03:09.230 --> 00:03:13.870
and the arrival of the
second World War in 1940.

00:03:14.890 --> 00:03:17.390
And so in that time,

00:03:17.390 --> 00:03:19.190
there was of course the first World War,

00:03:19.190 --> 00:03:22.830
but also various strains of antisemitism

00:03:22.830 --> 00:03:26.160
that targeted these families in public

00:03:26.160 --> 00:03:29.610
and always by name and
that sort of hounded them

00:03:29.610 --> 00:03:31.773
and that they could never really escape,

00:03:32.820 --> 00:03:35.350
given that they were as I
mentioned, this sort of elite,

00:03:35.350 --> 00:03:40.350
that basically they were the
custodians of the French Jewish

00:03:41.360 --> 00:03:42.610
experience at that time.

00:03:42.610 --> 00:03:44.240
And by that I mean,

00:03:44.240 --> 00:03:48.020
they were the heads of
the communal organizations

00:03:48.020 --> 00:03:51.840
such as the (speaks foreign language)

00:03:55.427 --> 00:03:59.310
And they were members of
the French parliament,

00:03:59.310 --> 00:04:01.100
like the Reinach family was,

00:04:01.100 --> 00:04:03.920
the big defenders of
Dreyfus during the affair,

00:04:03.920 --> 00:04:05.590
also the Reinach,

00:04:05.590 --> 00:04:09.260
they were the governors
and philanthropists

00:04:09.260 --> 00:04:12.810
that supported cultural
life all across France,

00:04:12.810 --> 00:04:14.980
such as the Rothschild

00:04:14.980 --> 00:04:17.320
and Alphonse the Rothschild in particular.

00:04:17.320 --> 00:04:20.210
And so, they are at the heart

00:04:20.210 --> 00:04:25.210
of this very particular
experience in a fraught moment.

00:04:25.570 --> 00:04:28.250
And so, I mean the complicated thing

00:04:28.250 --> 00:04:29.770
is that both

00:04:34.829 --> 00:04:36.020
the period of the Dreyfus affair

00:04:36.020 --> 00:04:40.000
and the second World War and
then also just the sort of

00:04:40.000 --> 00:04:42.370
Jewish experience in France in general,

00:04:42.370 --> 00:04:45.210
are such well-trodden
territories in scholarship

00:04:45.210 --> 00:04:46.150
as all of us know,

00:04:46.150 --> 00:04:49.280
and of course, I'm here today
with with Maurice and Alice

00:04:49.280 --> 00:04:52.240
both of whom have written
so much and so well

00:04:52.240 --> 00:04:53.253
about all of that.

00:04:54.380 --> 00:04:56.040
When I was looking at all of this stuff

00:04:56.040 --> 00:05:01.040
I struggled to answer
the question of what was,

00:05:01.259 --> 00:05:06.259
what is left to say and what
is sort of a contribution

00:05:06.980 --> 00:05:10.250
to be made in the research I was doing.

00:05:10.250 --> 00:05:15.250
I think after reading everything I could

00:05:15.750 --> 00:05:19.989
and after kind of
brainstorming about what to say

00:05:19.989 --> 00:05:21.080
about

00:05:22.390 --> 00:05:23.820
this community,

00:05:23.820 --> 00:05:25.590
it occurred to me that,

00:05:25.590 --> 00:05:29.040
pretty much every single
one of these great families

00:05:30.890 --> 00:05:35.890
had bequeath a collection
of art or a family home

00:05:38.180 --> 00:05:42.260
intended to be a sort of private
museum to the French state

00:05:42.260 --> 00:05:44.317
in the 1920s and thirties.

00:05:44.317 --> 00:05:47.560
So, before the war, before the Holocaust,

00:05:47.560 --> 00:05:49.850
before the ending that we all know.

00:05:49.850 --> 00:05:54.850
And these donations really
seemed to me very interesting

00:05:55.450 --> 00:05:57.690
because on the one hand,

00:05:57.690 --> 00:06:02.690
they offer sort of multiple
avenues of interpretation.

00:06:02.990 --> 00:06:07.590
On the one hand, as collections,

00:06:07.590 --> 00:06:12.590
they are expression and then
here I'm indebted to the phrase

00:06:12.770 --> 00:06:15.410
of the art historian and you
go name their self portraits

00:06:15.410 --> 00:06:16.870
of the collectors themselves, right?

00:06:16.870 --> 00:06:17.810
And collections,

00:06:17.810 --> 00:06:20.680
there's an enormous literature about this.

00:06:20.680 --> 00:06:22.168
Collections exist at the intersection

00:06:22.168 --> 00:06:25.023
of the psychological and the social,

00:06:26.647 --> 00:06:30.000
there is sort of what a
collector chooses to collect

00:06:30.000 --> 00:06:32.700
corresponds to some sort
of subjective aspect

00:06:32.700 --> 00:06:34.390
of his or her person,

00:06:34.390 --> 00:06:36.623
whether it's a childhood memory,

00:06:38.578 --> 00:06:39.411
whether it's a sense of aspiration,

00:06:41.430 --> 00:06:42.993
whether it's some kind of strange,

00:06:44.160 --> 00:06:46.108
kind of personal desire or,

00:06:46.108 --> 00:06:48.670
to use the phrase that I love

00:06:48.670 --> 00:06:50.660
from Susan Stewart's
"Study of Collecting,"

00:06:50.660 --> 00:06:52.363
it's a sense of longing.

00:06:53.370 --> 00:06:56.070
And so there's all of that
sort of hyper personal,

00:06:56.070 --> 00:07:01.070
hyper individualistic
components to collecting.

00:07:01.440 --> 00:07:03.710
But then also given that

00:07:03.710 --> 00:07:07.300
this is a network of
interconnected families

00:07:07.300 --> 00:07:11.380
and collectors who have
a very similar background

00:07:11.380 --> 00:07:12.943
in the same moment,

00:07:14.160 --> 00:07:15.750
there is also the social aspect.

00:07:15.750 --> 00:07:17.840
And so, what collectors choose to collect

00:07:17.840 --> 00:07:19.040
in a given moment in time

00:07:19.040 --> 00:07:21.880
is often a function of what is in style

00:07:21.880 --> 00:07:23.530
in that particular time.

00:07:23.530 --> 00:07:26.550
And what the style is in a given moment

00:07:26.550 --> 00:07:27.930
is also very interesting.

00:07:27.930 --> 00:07:29.840
So, for example,

00:07:29.840 --> 00:07:32.796
in the Victorian era there
was the revival of the Gothic

00:07:32.796 --> 00:07:35.760
in Fantasia France.

00:07:35.760 --> 00:07:39.360
The sort of very invoke style

00:07:39.360 --> 00:07:41.625
was the Ancien Regime

00:07:41.625 --> 00:07:46.250
popularized by the likes of
Julia and Roman Gaul and others.

00:07:46.250 --> 00:07:49.020
And I'll talk a little bit
more about that in a minute

00:07:49.020 --> 00:07:51.570
when I have my slides up and running

00:07:51.570 --> 00:07:52.970
but I thought I would just sort of,

00:07:52.970 --> 00:07:55.750
briefly introduce the sort
of general arc of the book

00:07:55.750 --> 00:07:56.890
before we go any further.

00:07:56.890 --> 00:08:00.010
So, I was always really
interested in these collections

00:08:00.010 --> 00:08:01.113
and what they tell us.

00:08:02.566 --> 00:08:04.060
And again, there's no simple answer

00:08:04.060 --> 00:08:05.468
because we're talking about,

00:08:05.468 --> 00:08:09.100
very complicated nuanced individuals

00:08:09.100 --> 00:08:12.350
who have any number of different
perspectives and opinions

00:08:12.350 --> 00:08:14.886
on the moment that they're living in.

00:08:14.886 --> 00:08:15.760
But, as a group,

00:08:15.760 --> 00:08:17.030
and I should say also that

00:08:17.030 --> 00:08:18.796
each of the families that I consider,

00:08:18.796 --> 00:08:23.796
are intermarried and related
through various channels

00:08:25.300 --> 00:08:28.940
and so to borrow Loren
Berman's famous phrase

00:08:28.940 --> 00:08:30.910
from the seventies, "It's
really a cousin hood."

00:08:30.910 --> 00:08:34.800
I mean, they're really all
a big family in a sense.

00:08:34.800 --> 00:08:36.560
And so, they all knew each other,

00:08:36.560 --> 00:08:41.560
they were all thinking fairly
similarly about their legacies

00:08:42.700 --> 00:08:47.700
and this for so many of
them was their legacy.

00:08:47.980 --> 00:08:52.890
And so I think that that is
quite rich for interpretation.

00:08:52.890 --> 00:08:55.313
And I should say that,

00:08:56.720 --> 00:08:59.690
it's interesting because
the characters in the book,

00:08:59.690 --> 00:09:01.460
they are all from Jewish families,

00:09:01.460 --> 00:09:05.070
but they have a very diverse
array of relationships

00:09:05.070 --> 00:09:06.920
to Judaism and Jewishness.

00:09:06.920 --> 00:09:11.510
And some are very invested
in religious observance.

00:09:11.510 --> 00:09:15.110
Some are communal leaders as I mentioned,

00:09:15.110 --> 00:09:17.737
the Rothschild were at one
point ahead of the costliest

00:09:17.737 --> 00:09:20.530
Theodore Reinach who is a
big character in the book

00:09:20.530 --> 00:09:23.620
and one of my favorites was,

00:09:23.620 --> 00:09:28.460
a great sort of theorist of Franco-Judaism

00:09:28.460 --> 00:09:30.587
following in the footsteps of the likes of

00:09:30.587 --> 00:09:33.500
the hallevies, a generation before.

00:09:33.500 --> 00:09:37.270
And he founded the
precursor to the synagogue

00:09:37.270 --> 00:09:38.690
that still exists in Paris,

00:09:38.690 --> 00:09:41.340
the liberal synagogue
(speaks foreign language)

00:09:41.340 --> 00:09:45.410
for those of you who may
be familiar with that.

00:09:45.410 --> 00:09:48.470
And so there's that side,

00:09:48.470 --> 00:09:49.446
but then there are others who had

00:09:49.446 --> 00:09:52.529
no sense of Jewish
identity at the end at all.

00:09:52.529 --> 00:09:54.510
And who had converted,

00:09:54.510 --> 00:09:58.100
who had married non Jews,

00:09:58.100 --> 00:10:00.410
who had kind of distanced themselves

00:10:00.410 --> 00:10:04.870
from the tradition of their forefathers.

00:10:04.870 --> 00:10:07.100
And it's interesting that
at the end of the day

00:10:07.100 --> 00:10:09.100
of course the nature of their identities

00:10:09.100 --> 00:10:10.923
it's not up to them to arbitrate,

00:10:11.940 --> 00:10:13.150
during the second World War

00:10:13.150 --> 00:10:15.700
with Nazi occupation in the Holocaust,

00:10:15.700 --> 00:10:18.873
but, it is a sort of,

00:10:20.270 --> 00:10:23.110
I realized that on some level
it's a strange grouping.

00:10:23.110 --> 00:10:27.190
But, I do think that for so many of them

00:10:27.190 --> 00:10:30.290
going through the same lived experience

00:10:30.290 --> 00:10:33.540
in the same very fraught
complicated moment,

00:10:33.540 --> 00:10:36.580
the collections really
are a profound statement

00:10:36.580 --> 00:10:37.470
when taken together.

00:10:37.470 --> 00:10:38.440
And I'll show you a little bit

00:10:38.440 --> 00:10:40.320
more about what I mean
by that in a minute.

00:10:40.320 --> 00:10:41.153
But just,

00:10:42.990 --> 00:10:45.650
in general what I try to
do here with this book

00:10:45.650 --> 00:10:50.400
is essentially to look at these
collections as expressions

00:10:56.186 --> 00:10:58.800
of a kind of evolving
French Jewish identity,

00:10:58.800 --> 00:11:01.900
and here I think a lot
about Maurice's work

00:11:01.900 --> 00:11:03.610
on the Israelites.

00:11:04.890 --> 00:11:07.810
Maurice has considered this
in the terms of fiction,

00:11:07.810 --> 00:11:11.250
as a laboratory for the
construction of that very French,

00:11:11.250 --> 00:11:13.370
very 19th century Jewish identity.

00:11:13.370 --> 00:11:17.040
And I try to do the same
through the realm of material,

00:11:17.040 --> 00:11:19.230
culture, and art and objects.

00:11:19.230 --> 00:11:23.210
And I hope we can talk a
little bit more about that too

00:11:23.210 --> 00:11:25.380
as we go along.

00:11:25.380 --> 00:11:28.270
And finally, if the book
has an argument I would say

00:11:28.270 --> 00:11:32.160
it's essentially three things.

00:11:32.160 --> 00:11:36.219
On the one hand, it's
that the antisemitism,

00:11:36.219 --> 00:11:39.220
and I should say also I'm
not the first to go here.

00:11:39.220 --> 00:11:43.610
There's been a lot of excellent
scholarship on this front

00:11:43.610 --> 00:11:45.330
from the likes of Tom Stammers

00:11:45.330 --> 00:11:47.540
at Durham university and others,

00:11:47.540 --> 00:11:51.160
that the antisemitism
of both and this Jack

00:11:51.160 --> 00:11:54.580
was at least to a certain extent,

00:11:54.580 --> 00:11:57.710
largely aesthetic or material in nature.

00:11:57.710 --> 00:11:59.000
And by that I mean,

00:11:59.000 --> 00:12:03.710
it was a hatred of Jews
expressed through the language

00:12:03.710 --> 00:12:08.100
of art and objects and things
and that's very important.

00:12:08.100 --> 00:12:11.160
And then the second part
of that argument is that,

00:12:11.160 --> 00:12:14.363
for many of these very established,

00:12:19.629 --> 00:12:20.462
important French Jewish
families in this time,

00:12:20.462 --> 00:12:23.500
their response was also
rendered in the language

00:12:23.500 --> 00:12:24.640
of objects and things.

00:12:24.640 --> 00:12:26.810
And that is sort of how
I see these collections.

00:12:26.810 --> 00:12:30.050
As rejoinders to the
sort of hateful invective

00:12:30.050 --> 00:12:31.860
they could never escape.

00:12:31.860 --> 00:12:35.410
And finally I really do believe

00:12:35.410 --> 00:12:38.473
that these collections are to some extent,

00:12:41.280 --> 00:12:44.500
portraits of individual sensibilities,

00:12:44.500 --> 00:12:49.500
but also expressions of a
durable French Jewish identity.

00:12:51.870 --> 00:12:54.420
That was not mutually exclusive,

00:12:54.420 --> 00:12:55.740
and that was very compatible

00:12:55.740 --> 00:12:58.070
and that presented
Frenchness and Jewishness,

00:12:58.070 --> 00:13:01.693
perhaps even as symbiotic
extensions of each other,

00:13:04.539 --> 00:13:05.372
and again, an extremely fraught moment

00:13:05.372 --> 00:13:06.543
in which antisemitism was rampant

00:13:06.543 --> 00:13:09.520
and there was no escape.

00:13:09.520 --> 00:13:13.172
I think very much often of the line

00:13:13.172 --> 00:13:17.380
toward the beginning of Proust's,

00:13:17.380 --> 00:13:22.380
in search of lost time when
Marcel invites a Jewish friend

00:13:22.590 --> 00:13:25.450
over to play after school one
day or something like that,

00:13:25.450 --> 00:13:28.160
and his grandfather hums certain tunes.

00:13:28.160 --> 00:13:31.650
I believe even from the
holiday opera Lez verse

00:13:31.650 --> 00:13:33.450
or something else that could be mistaken.

00:13:33.450 --> 00:13:34.770
But the point is,

00:13:34.770 --> 00:13:39.770
when the small Jewish boy
comes over to his house

00:13:39.820 --> 00:13:41.310
the grandfather hums these tunes

00:13:41.310 --> 00:13:43.000
that sort of marked this difference.

00:13:43.000 --> 00:13:44.900
And the point of the book in a way is that

00:13:44.900 --> 00:13:46.810
all of these collectors lived their lives

00:13:46.810 --> 00:13:49.360
to the sound of that music to some extent.

00:13:49.360 --> 00:13:53.760
And it was something that
they tried to overcome

00:13:53.760 --> 00:13:57.350
and that I really think that

00:13:57.350 --> 00:14:00.390
the art and the objects
that they collected

00:14:00.390 --> 00:14:03.880
as donations to the state
were meant to put an end

00:14:03.880 --> 00:14:04.980
to that once and for all

00:14:04.980 --> 00:14:06.780
and to demonstrate

00:14:06.780 --> 00:14:09.280
not only a Jewish fidelity
to the French Republic,

00:14:09.280 --> 00:14:10.113
but

00:14:12.671 --> 00:14:15.439
a sort of profound sense of belonging

00:14:15.439 --> 00:14:19.840
and also of contribution to
French cultural patrimony.

00:14:19.840 --> 00:14:21.439
So, with that said,

00:14:21.439 --> 00:14:25.160
I will now show some slides
and sort of walk you through

00:14:25.160 --> 00:14:27.780
a little bit of what's in the book.

00:14:27.780 --> 00:14:29.980
So let me just do that real quick.

