00:55:52.982 --> 00:55:55.582
because I think it's meant to show

00:55:55.582 --> 00:56:00.250
that this immigrant family is as proud

00:56:00.250 --> 00:56:02.910
of French as proud of
France as anyone else

00:56:04.076 --> 00:56:06.810
and as equally able to
understand its genius.

00:56:06.810 --> 00:56:09.430
So I don't know, that's
my, I think that that's one

00:56:09.430 --> 00:56:12.630
of the things I loved about
your book is you really

00:56:12.630 --> 00:56:16.130
give such a nuanced understanding

00:56:16.130 --> 00:56:18.972
of French Jewish identity,
you know, so you could look

00:56:18.972 --> 00:56:22.140
at these people and on
the surface, and it's true

00:56:22.140 --> 00:56:25.512
that some of them like
be it face to come on DOE

00:56:25.512 --> 00:56:28.780
but what you really show is that, you know

00:56:28.780 --> 00:56:30.330
what might seem like a kind

00:56:30.330 --> 00:56:33.859
of disaffection from Judaism
is really just, you know

00:56:33.859 --> 00:56:36.750
that there were different ways

00:56:36.750 --> 00:56:39.270
of expressing those affiliations and yes

00:56:39.270 --> 00:56:42.217
they change over the
course of the 19th century.

00:56:42.217 --> 00:56:45.440
They become less traditional,
but it's not a rejection

00:56:46.315 --> 00:56:47.234
- Of it.

00:56:47.234 --> 00:56:48.206
And it's like another way

00:56:48.206 --> 00:56:49.490
of being just another way of being Jewish.

00:56:49.490 --> 00:56:51.690
- Yeah. And through this culture

00:56:51.690 --> 00:56:54.910
I just thought that was just
very, very beautifully done.

00:56:54.910 --> 00:56:58.530
So last question, you
know, that someone's pose

00:56:58.530 --> 00:57:01.493
